(For those who loved across the wreckage of the world,) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 475 Tm
(and for the children born of silence.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 415 Tm
(CHAPTER ONE) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 355 Tm
(The snow had been falling since before dawn, a slow and deliberate descent that turned the streets of Warsaw into something almost beautiful, almost clean. Helena Wojtera watched it from the window of the fourth-floor flat on ulica Mokotowska, her breath fogging the glass, her fingers curled around the edge of the curtain as though she might tear it away if she held on too tightly. Below, the street was empty save for a dogsome mangrel thing, brown and rib-thinpicking its way through the drifts with its nose pressed to the ground, searching for scraps that the snow had already buried.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 335 Tm
(She checked the clock on the wall. Seven minutes past six. Marysia would need to wake soon. The girl had been coughing in the night, a dry, rattling sound that had kept Helena awake until nearly three, lying on her pallet beside the child's bed, pressing her palm against Marysia's small back and feeling the heat radiate through the thin cotton of her nightgown. By morning the fever had broken, but Helena had not slept. She rarely did, not deeply, not anymore. Sleep in occupied Warsaw was a shallow country, a place you visited with one eye open and your ears tuned to the sound of boots on cobblestone.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315 Tm
(She let the curtain fall and moved to the small stove in the corner of the room, crouching to feed it with the last of yesterday's coal. The fire caught reluctantly, spitting and smoking before settling into a sullen glow. The flat was cold. It had been cold since November, when the last proper delivery of fuel had come and the price had tripled anyway. Helena had stopped counting the zlotys. She counted calories now, and degrees, and the number of hours between one roundup and the next.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 295 Tm
(In the other roomreally just an alcove separated by a threadbare curtainMarysia stirred. Helena heard the small cough, the rustle of blankets, and then the girl's voice, still thick with sleep:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 275 Tm
("Mama?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 255 Tm
("I'm here, kochanie." Helena crossed the room and drew the curtain aside. Marysia was sitting up in bed, her dark hair tangled, her eyes too bright and too large in her thin face. She was nine years old and she looked seven, or perhaps elevenchildren in wartime aged in strange and uneven ways, their bodies growing slow while their eyes grew old. "How do you feel?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 235 Tm
("My throat hurts a little.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 215 Tm
(Helena pressed the back of her hand to the girl's forehead. The skin was warm but not feverish. "Drink." She held out the cup of water she'd left on the nightstand. Marysia drank obediently, then lay back against the pillow.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 195 Tm
("Are you going to school today?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175 Tm
(Helena's chest tightened. School. The word had become a fiction they both maintained, a comfortable lie. There was no school for Polish children in the Generalgouvernement. Not really. There were secret lessons in cellars and back rooms, taught by teachers who risked imprisonment or death for the privilege of passing on the forbidden inheritance of their language and history. Marysia attended one such room, three streets over, run by a former professor named Pani Kowalska who had lost her husband in the September campaign and her two sons to camps she would not name aloud.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 155 Tm
("Not today," Helena said. "You'll stay home.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 135 Tm
(Marysia's face fell. "But I feel better") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 115 Tm
("You stayed in bed all week with the cough. One more day." Helena smoothed the girl's hair. "Pani Kowalska will understand. Rest today, and tomorrow I'll take you myself.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 95 Tm
(The girl nodded, though Helena could see the disappointment. Marysia was at that age where she wanted desperately to be normal, to be a child among other children, to carry a satchel and complain about lessons and trade bread crusts for marbles. Helena had no way to give her this. She could not give her normal. She could give her only safety, and even that was a currency that devalued daily.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 75 Tm
(She returned to the kitchen alcove and set about preparing what passed for breakfast. Breaddark, dense, barely risenthat she'd traded two hours of mending to obtain. A thin smear of margarine. A cup of barley water that she called tea. She ate standing, as she always did, one hand on the windowsill, watching the street below for any sign of movement that wasn't the wind or the thin dog.) Tj
