(The routine was essential. It was what kept them alive. Every morning, Helena performed the same ritual of observation: the street, the courtyard, the building across the way. She noted which windows were dark, which doors had new locks, which faces appeared at familiar hours. She had mapped the rhythms of the blockthe old man who swept his stoop at seven-fifteen, the woman who hung laundry on Tuesdays regardless of weather, the young man from the second floor who hadn't been seen in three weeks and whose absence was a silence that spoke louder than any scream.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 525 Tm
(Today the street was quiet. This was unusual. On a Wednesday morning at this hour there were usually at least a few figures hurrying to the tram stop, heads bowed against the snow, shoulders hunched. But the street was empty. Helena pressed her face closer to the glass.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 505 Tm
(Then she heard it. Not the street, but the next street overulica Nowowiejska. A sound she knew too well. The clatter of truck engines. The sharp crack of orders. And underneath it, rising and falling like a thing alive: the sound of people screaming.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 485 Tm
(A apanka. A roundup.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 465 Tm
(Her body moved before her mind could catch up. She was already crossing to the alcove, pulling back the curtain, her hands finding Marysia's arms and pulling her upright. The girl's eyes were wide with the terror that all Polish children learned before they learned to read.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 445 Tm
("Mama") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 425 Tm
("Listen to me." Helena's voice was steady, the voice she used for the worst moments, the voice she had forged in the first terrible days of the occupation and had kept sharp ever since. "We need to go. Now. The back stairs. Do you understand?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 405 Tm
("The front") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 385 Tm
("No. The back. Get dressed. Everything you have. The coat. The scarf. Wrap it around your face.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 365 Tm
(Marysia was moving now, small hands fumbling with buttons, with the rough wool of her coat. Helena shoved her feet into her own boots, grabbed the bag she kept packed by the doora bag that always contained forged papers, a change of clothes for each of them, a small sum of zlotys rolled into a tight cylinder, a silver locket that was the last thing of value she owned. She had prepared for this moment every day for four years. She had rehearsed it in her sleep.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 345 Tm
(But rehearsing and living were different countries, and the border between them was crossed in a single heartbeat.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 325 Tm
(They made it to the landing. The stairwell was darkthe electricity had been cut to the building two days agoand Helena moved by feel, one hand on the banister, the other gripping Marysia's wrist. Down three flights. The back door opened into a narrow alley that ran between the building and the wall of the next courtyard. Helena pushed it open and stepped out into the cold.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 305 Tm
(The alley was clear. They moved quickly, heads down, boots crunching on the snow. Helena knew this route. She had walked it a hundred times in her mind, mapping each turn, each hiding place, each possible dead end. Left at the end of the alley. Through the gap in the wall that separated the two courtyards. Across the second courtyard, past the coal bins, to the front gate that opened onto) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 285 Tm
(She stopped.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 265 Tm
(Ulica Wilcza was not empty.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 245 Tm
(A truck sat idling in the middle of the street, its canvas-covered back open, soldiers standing in the bed with rifles held casually across their chests. On either side of the vehicle, a cordon of men in green-grey uniforms formed a tightening perimeter, sweeping civilians from doorways and dragging them from behind walls. A woman with a baby. An old man in slippers. Two boys no older than twelve. All of them being herded toward the truck like cattle.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 225 Tm
(And at the center of it, directing the operation with calm, precise gestures, was an officer.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 205 Tm
(Helena pulled Marysia behind the coal bins, her heart hammering. They were not yet seen. The cordon was still fifty meters away, tightening its arc. She could feel Marysia trembling against her legs, could hear the girl's breathing go thin and fast.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 185 Tm
(Think. Think.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 165 Tm
(The alley behind them. Could they go back? Nothe truck was on the main street, but the side streets would have secondary cordons. This was a proper operation, a large-scale sweep of the district. She could see at least twenty soldiers, perhaps more. This was not a random terror action. This was methodical.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 145 Tm
(A sound broke through her calculations. Marysia cougheda single, sharp, rattling cough that cut through the morning air like a bell.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 125 Tm
(Helena's blood turned to ice.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 105 Tm
(One of the soldiers turned. He looked directly at the coal bins. He said something to the officer.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 85 Tm
(The officer turned.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 65 Tm
(And Helena saw his face.) Tj
