(The world did not stop. It did not slow. The snow continued to fall, the truck continued to idle, the civilians continued to cry out. But inside Helena's skull, something collapseda wall she had spent ten years building, a dam she had constructed stone by stone from silence and determination and the sheer animal will to forget.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 515 Tm
(Because she knew that face.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 495 Tm
(She knew the line of that jaw, the particular angle of those cheekbones, the color of those eyesa grey-blue that in the Munich sunlight had looked almost silver. She knew the way his dark blond hair fell just above his collar, the same way it had when he was twenty-three and leaning over a seminar table, arguing with her about Heine. She knew that face from a thousand mornings in a small flat on Amalienstrasse, from evenings in the Englischer Garten, from a night in September when he had knelt on a bridge over the Isar and held out a ring.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 475 Tm
(Jochen.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 455 Tm
(Jochen von Brandt.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 435 Tm
(He stood there in his greatcoat, his leather gloves, the Iron Cross at his throat, and he was looking directly at her. The recognition came across the distance like a physical thinga blow to the chest that drove the breath from her lungs.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 415 Tm
(For a momentone of those moments that contains within it the collapse and reconstruction of an entire universehe simply stared. His mouth opened slightly. His hand, raised mid-gesture to one of his men, froze in the air.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 395 Tm
(Then he began to move toward her.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 375 Tm
("Mama," Marysia whispered. "Mama, who") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 355 Tm
("Run.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 335 Tm
(Helena grabbed the girl's hand and pulled her away from the bins, away from the alley, away from the only escape route that had existed. She ran. Not because she had a plan, not because she could see a path to safety, but because the alternative was to stand still, and standing still meant letting him catch them, and letting him catch them meant) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315 Tm
(She didn't know what it meant. That was the terror of it. She didn't know what he would do. She didn't know what he was.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 295 Tm
(They ran through the courtyard, through the gap in the wall, back into the first alley. Behind them, she heard the officer's voiceshouting now, not in the clipped tones of military command but in something rawer, something that sounded almost like a name.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 275 Tm
("Helena!") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 255 Tm
(She stumbled. Marysia's hand jerked in hers. The girl was small and fast but her legs were short and the snow was deep and she was still recovering from illness and she was slowing Helena down and Helena was) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 235 Tm
("Let go, Mama," Marysia gasped. "Let go and run faster") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 215 Tm
("No!" Helena's voice cracked like a whip. "No, we stay together. We always stay together.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 195 Tm
(They reached the back door of the building. Helena fumbled with the latch, got it open, pushed Marysia inside. The stairwell. Up. She could hear boots behind themheavy, rhythmic, coming fast. She seized Marysia's hand again and they climbed, two flights, three, Helena's lungs burning, the girl sobbing with exertion.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175 Tm
(Fourth floor. The door. Keys. Her hands were shaking so badly she dropped them once, and Marysiablessed, brilliant childsnatched them from the floor and shoved them into the lock herself.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 155 Tm
(Inside. The bolt. The chain. Helena threw her back against the door as though her body alone could hold it.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 135 Tm
(Silence.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 115 Tm
(Then: footsteps on the stairs. Slow now. Deliberate.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 95 Tm
(They stopped outside the door.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 75 Tm
(A knock. Not the battering knock of a raid. A single, measured rap. As though this were a social call. As though this were a man calling on an old friend.) Tj
