1 0 0 1 50 535 Tm
(Munich, October 1930.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 515 Tm
(The city announced itself to Helena Wojtera through the window of a third-class train carriage, appearing first as a smear of light on the horizonthe pale gold of gas lamps reflected in low cloudsand then resolving, as the train slowed, into something vast and solid and terrifyingly real. She pressed her forehead against the cold glass and watched the suburbs unfold: factories with their chimneystacks breathing white plumes into the autumn sky, tenement blocks grey as the Bavarian stone they were built from, and then, suddenly, the domes and spires of the city center rising above the rooftops like the towers of a fairy tale drawn in ink.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 495 Tm
(She was twenty-one years old. She had a suitcase full of clothes, a letter of admission to the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität, and exactly forty-seven Reichsmarks sewn into the lining of her coat. She spoke German wellher father had insisted on it, her father who believed that languages were armor and that a girl who could move between tongues could move between worldsbut she had never been beyond the borders of Poland. The longest journey she had taken before this was the train from Kraków to Warsaw, and that had been a matter of hours, not the overnight passage that had brought her here, through the flat lands of Silesia and across the frontier into a country that had, within her lifetime, attempted to erase hers.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 475 Tm
(She told herself this didn't matter. She told herself she was not Polish in the parochial senseshe was a citizen of the intellectual world, a subject of reason and letters and the republic of ideas. The university would be neutral territory. The professors would not care about borders. The books would not ask her passport.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 455 Tm
(She was naive. She knew this even then, even as she told herself otherwise. But naivety, in the young, is not ignoranceit is the courageous refusal to let knowledge curdle into despair.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 435 Tm
(The train deposited her at the Hauptbahnhof, and she stepped onto the platform into a world of noise and movement and the particular quality of Bavarian autumn light that made everything look gilded, even the soot-stained walls of the station. She stood with her suitcase and her coat and her forty-seven Reichsmarks and she felt, for the first time, the full weight of her solitude.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 415 Tm
(The room she had been allocatedthrough a letter from a Polish aid society that assisted students studying abroadwas in Schwabing, the artists' quarter, in a building on Amalienstrasse that leaned slightly to the left, as though it had spent too long listening to the wind. The room was small: a bed, a desk, a washstand, a window that looked out over a courtyard cluttered with bicycles and laundry lines. The wallpaper was peeling. The radiator hissed like a creature in pain. It was perfect.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 395 Tm
(Helena set her suitcase on the bed, removed her coat, and stood in the center of the room and breathed. She was here. She was actually here. In Munich. In Germany. At the university where Richard Wagner had once walked, where Max Planck held forth, where Rainer Maria Rilke had written letters that made her weep. She was here, and the world was wide, and she was a woman who could read in four languages and write in three, and she was going to study philosophy and literature and she was going to become someone.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 375 Tm
(The first week was a blur of registration and orientation and the particular humiliation of standing in a foreign language and not understanding every word. Her German was bookish, precise, learned from grammar texts and her father's careful instruction. The German spoken by the other students was something else entirelya living, breathing thing that moved at speed, that swallowed its endings, that was peppered with Bavarian idioms she had never encountered. She sat in lectures and caught perhaps half of what was said. She took furious notes. She went back to her room each evening and re-read them and filled the gaps with a dictionary.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 355 Tm
(The other students, for the most part, ignored her. She was a curiositya Polish woman in a department that was ninety percent male, and a foreign one at that. The German students were polite but distant. They formed their own clusters, spoke their own shorthand, laughed at jokes she didn't understand. She ate alone in the student canteen, sitting at the edge of a long table, her eyes down, her book open, performing the universal pantomime of a person who does not wish to be disturbed, even though what she wishedwhat she ached for, with an ache that sat in her throat like a stonewas for someone to look up and see her and speak.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 335 Tm
(It happened on the eighth day.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315 Tm
(The seminar was on nineteenth-century German poetryHeine, Hölderlin, Kleisttaught by a professor named Albrecht whose lectures were legendary for their brilliance and their unpredictability. He did not read from notes. He paced. He quoted from memory, entire stanzas, his eyes closed, his hands moving as though he were conducting an invisible orchestra. He demanded that his students argue, challenge, contradict. "Philosophy," he told them on the first day, "is not a museum. It is a battlefield.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 295 Tm
(Helena had read all of Heine. Her father's library had a complete edition, bound in green leather, and she had spent the summer before her departure reading it cover to cover, marking passages in pencil, arguing with the margins. Heine was her poetshe felt him in her bones, in the particular ache of exile that ran through his verses like a current, in the way he could hold beauty and bitterness in the same hand and refuse to let either go.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 275 Tm
(That day, the seminar was on Buch der Lieder. Professor Albrecht was in rare form, moving through the poems with a kind of electric intensity, pausing at a particular stanza from Lyrisches Intermezzo"In the lovely month of May, when all the buds were springing"and asking the class to account for the tension between the poem's surface sweetness and its undertow of despair.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 255 Tm
(A voice answered. A man's voice, from the far side of the room. Helena looked up.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 235 Tm
(He was sitting near the window, turned slightly in his chair, one leg crossed over the other, a pencil held loosely between his fingers. He was tall, even sitting downyou could see it in the length of his torso, the breadth of his shoulders beneath his tweed jacket. His hair was dark blond, cut short but not severely, falling just above his collar in a way that suggested he ran his hands through it often. His face was angularhigh cheekbones, a strong jawbut his eyes were what arrested her. They were grey-blue, the color of winter sky over a lake, and they had a quality she would later learn to name: an intensity that was not aggression but attention, a way of looking at you as though you were the most interesting text he had ever encountered.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 215 Tm
("The tension," he said, "is not between sweetness and despair. It is between memory and desire. The poem is not about what happened. It is about what the speaker wishes had happened. The May is not a real May. It is a May that exists only in the imagination, and the despair comes from knowing the difference.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 195 Tm
(Professor Albrecht's eyes lit. "And how do you know this, Herr") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175 Tm
("Von Brandt. Jochen von Brandt.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 155 Tm
("Herr von Brandt. How do you know this?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 135 Tm
("Because," the young man said, and now he turned his head and his eyes found Helena across the roomnot her specifically, not yet, but the general direction of the foreign students, as though his argument needed to reach everyone"because the poem is written in the past tense. Everything in it has already happened. But the speaker cannot stop reconstructing it. He cannot stop going back and remaking it. The buds are always springing. The nightingale is always singing. The beloved is always about to appear. That is not memory. That is obsession. That is a man standing in the wreckage of his own heart and refusing to leave the scene.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 115 Tm
(The room was quiet. Helena felt something shift in her chesta click, like a key turning in a lock she hadn't known was there.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 95 Tm
(She did not speak that day. She sat and listened and took notes and tried not to stare. But when the seminar ended and the students rose and shuffled toward the door, she watched himwatched him pack his books with a leisurely precision, watched him nod to a friend, watched him walk out into the corridor with the particular gait of someone who occupies space without apology.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 75 Tm
(She told herself she was not interested. She told herself this was dangerous territorya Polish woman and a German man, in a country whose relationship to hers was written in blood and treaty and centuries of grievance. She told herself she had come here to study, not to become entangled.) Tj
