(She told herself many things in those early days. She would come to regret all of them.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 505 Tm
(He found her three days later, in the university library.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 485 Tm
(She was in the reading room, surrounded by stacks of books on romantic aesthetics, making notes in a ledger she had purchased from a stationer on Ludwigstrasse. Her pen moved in the precise, slanted hand her father had drilled into herletters that leaned forward as though they were always trying to reach the next word before the current one was finished.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 465 Tm
("Excuse me.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 445 Tm
(She looked up. He was standing at the end of her table, holding a book against his chest, looking at her with those grey-blue eyes.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 425 Tm
("You're the Polish girl. In Albrecht's seminar.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 405 Tm
("I am." She kept her voice neutral. "And you are the one who gave the lecture on Heine.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 385 Tm
(He smiled. It was not a charming smilenot calculated or performed. It was sudden, almost startled, as though the smile had happened before he'd decided to allow it. "I didn't give a lecture. I gave an opinion.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 365 Tm
("It sounded like a lecture.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 345 Tm
("Professor Albrecht thought so too. He told me afterward that I should either write my thoughts down or stop talking so much that I'm forced to have them.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 325 Tm
(Helena almost laughed. She caught it, held it back, but she felt it move through hera warm, involuntary thing, like sunlight through a window she hadn't realized was open.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 305 Tm
("Did you agree?" he asked. "With what I said? About the Heine?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 285 Tm
