1 0 0 1 50 255 Tm
(The weeks that followed were the brightest of her life. She knew this even as she lived themwhich is either a blessing or a curse, she would later think, depending on the hour and the bottle. She knew that what she had with Jochen was rare, was dangerous, was the kind of thing that the world did not generally permit to people like thema Polish woman and a German man, in a city that was becoming the capital of a movement that viewed her kind as subhuman. She knew all of this and she went forward anyway, because love, in its early and terrible fullness, is not a rational enterprise. It is a surrender. It is the white flag raised above the citadel of the self.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 235 Tm
(They met every day. In the library. In the garden. In the small cafés of Schwabing, where they sat over coffee and cigarette and argued about everythingabout philosophy and politics and the nature of beauty and the proper length of a line of poetry. He introduced her to his friendsa small circle of fellow students, mostly from the humanities, mostly liberal, mostly baffled by the political extremism that was growing around them like a weed. They accepted her with a warmth that surprised her, that made her feel, for the first time since arriving in Munich, that she might actually belong.) Tj
