(There she was. MarysiaKrystyna, on paperwas in the system. And if the processing continued according to the standard timeline, she would be moved within forty-eight hours to the transit camp at Pruszkow, and from there the children would be distributed to various fates: some to Germanization programs, some to labor camps, some to the ever-expanding machinery of disappearance that consumed the occupied territories like a fire that could not be fed.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 465 Tm
(Jochen closed the file. He sat back. He looked at the ceiling.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 445 Tm
(He could get her out. He had the authority. A signature, a stamp, an annotation in the marginrelease, error, administrative correctionand the child would be free. It would take five minutes. It would cost him nothing.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 425 Tm
(Except it would cost him everything. Because getting the child out would mean seeing Helena again. It would mean going back to that door. It would mean entering the sealed room and finding, inside it, everything he had spent ten years trying to forget.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 405 Tm
(He signed the release.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 365 Tm
(The knock came on the morning of the fourth day.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 345 Tm
(Helena had decided. She would go to Szucha. She would apply for Marysia's release. She would walk into the mouth of the beast and she would take her child back. She had spent the night composing her cover story in German, rehearsing it, testing it for weaknesses, hardening it like steel in a forge. She would be a Polish widowher husband had died in the September campaignsearching for her daughter. She would produce the papers. She would be calm, respectful, unremarkable. She would be invisible.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 325 Tm
(The knock interrupted her preparation. Three sharp rapsmilitary precision. Her stomach dropped.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 305 Tm
(She went to the door. She did not open it.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 285 Tm
("Frau Wojtera." The voice came through the wood, muffled but recognizable. "Please. I'm alone. I have something for you.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 265 Tm
(She pressed her ear to the door. She could hear him breathing. She could hear the faint creak of leather. She could hear the building around themthe radiator hissing, a door closing somewhere below, the muffled sound of a radio playing music from the other side of a wall.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 245 Tm
