(Ulica Szucha was a street that Helena had avoided for four years. It was the spine of the German administrative district in Warsawthe street along which the institutions of occupation arranged themselves like predators along a riverbank. The Gestapo headquarters occupied the corner building, a hulking structure of pre-war Polish modernism that had been converted, with characteristic German efficiency, into a place of interrogation and fear. The windows were blacked out. The entrance was guarded. The cars that came and went were dark-green and unmarked, and the people who were brought out of them were not the same people who had been put in.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 485 Tm
(Helena had heard the stories. Everyone in Warsaw had heard the stories. The cells in the basement where the interrogators worked with rubber truncheons and electrified wire. The holding rooms where prisoners waited for days without food or water, listening to the sounds from the other cells. The courtyard where, on certain mornings, the bodies were laid out for identificationbodies that had been alive the previous evening, bodies that had been people with names and families and plans for the following week.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 465 Tm
(She walked down this street now, holding her daughter's hand, and the cold entered her not through the skin but through something deepera cold that was not meteorological but existential, the cold of a woman who knows that she is walking toward the place where her worst fears live.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 445 Tm
(Marysia's hand was tight in hers. The girl was not speaking. She was walking with the careful, measured gait of a child who has learned that drawing attention is dangerous, that the correct response to fear is stillness, that the body must not betray what the mind is feeling. Helena envied her this discipline. She herself was trembling.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 425 Tm
(The processing office was in a building halfway down the streetnot the Gestapo headquarters itself, but an annex, a place where the bureaucratic business of occupation was conducted: the registration of civilians, the processing of detainees, the administration of labor assignments and resettlement orders. It was, in its way, more terrifying than the headquarters, because it was ordinary. It had a waiting room. It had a counter. It had forms and stamps and the mundane apparatus of administration. It was the banality of it that made it horriblethe way it transformed atrocity into paperwork, the way it made the destruction of human lives a matter of filing and routing and cross-referencing, as though the people who passed through its doors were not people at all but entries in a ledger.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 405 Tm
(Helena pushed open the door. The waiting room was half-fullwomen, mostly, with children, all of them quiet, all of them staring at the floor. A German clerk sat behind a high counter, writing in a ledger. He looked up when they entered. His eyes performed the same calculation as Herr von Brandt's had, ten years ago: female, child, Polish. Category: irrelevant.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 385 Tm
("Number?" he said, in German.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 365 Tm
("I was told to come for a release." Helena's German was flawless. The clerk's eyebrows rose slightlya Polish woman speaking German without accent was unexpected. "For the child detained in the Mokotow roundup. Krystyna Nowak.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 345 Tm
(He checked his ledger. His finger traced a line. "Nowak. Krystyna. Yes. She's been released. The authorization came from" He paused. His eyebrows rose higher. "From Oberleutnant von Brandt.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 325 Tm
(Helena's face did not change. She had expected this. She had prepared for this. But hearing the name spoken in this room, in this context, by this clerk who regarded it as nothing more than an administrative factthis was a different thing from hearing it whispered through a door.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 305 Tm
("My office is down the hall. Third door on the right." The clerk's voice was neutral, bored, the voice of a man who processes hundreds of releases a week and for whom this one is no different from any other. "The Oberleutnant said he would meet you there.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 285 Tm
(Helena nodded. She took Marysia's hand. They walked down the hall.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 265 Tm
(The corridor was long and white and brightly litthe kind of lighting that left no shadows, no corners, no place for secrets. The walls were bare except for a portrait of Hitler that hung at the far end, the Führer's eyes following them as they walked with the painted intensity of a man who believes, with absolute sincerity, that the world exists for his rearrangement.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 245 Tm
(The third door on the right was open. Jochen stood behind a desk, his hands resting on its surface, his uniform pressed, his cap on the chair beside him. He looked, in this light, older than he had in the street. The lines around his eyes were deeper. His face had lost the softness that remained in Helena's memorythe last traces of the young man who had argued about Heine in a sunlit seminar room. This was a man shaped by ten years of compromise, his face a landscape of small surrenders.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 225 Tm
(Marysia pressed against Helena's leg. Helena put her arm around the girl.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 205 Tm
("Thank you for coming," Jochen said. His voice was controlled, careful, the voice of a man speaking in a room where the walls had ears. "Please. Sit.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 185 Tm
(Helena did not sit. She stood in the center of the room, holding Marysia, and she looked at Jochen with an expression that was not angeranger would have been simpler, easier, more manageable. What she felt was something closer to grief: the grief of a woman looking at the ruins of the person she had loved and recognizing, in the rubble, the original architecture.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 165 Tm
("Where is she?" Helena said.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 145 Tm
("She's in the holding room down the hall. I've already processed the release. She just needs to be" He glanced at Marysia. His face changed. Something moved behind his eyesa recognition that went deeper than the physical, a recognition that bypassed the mind and went straight to some older, more primal part of him. He looked at the girlthis small, dark-haired, frightened childand his mouth opened slightly, and his hand came up as though to reach for something, and then he stopped himself.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 125 Tm
