1 0 0 1 50 215 Tm
(The weeks that followed the café meeting were a period of terrible, grinding tension. Helena went about her life with the mechanical precision of a woman who has divided herself into compartments that cannot be allowed to communicate. By day, she workedmending clothes for neighbors, translating documents for the underground, performing the endless small acts of survival that constituted existence in occupied Warsaw. By night, she lay awake and listened to Marysia breathing in the next room and thought about a man who was, against every rational imperative, becoming more present in her mind rather than less.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 195 Tm
(She told herself it was the shock. The encounter had been a traumaa detonation that had scattered the carefully arranged furniture of her inner life and left her picking through the wreckage, trying to remember what had belonged where. It was natural, she told herself, that the debris would keep surfacing. It was natural that she would think about him, that his face would appear in her mind at unexpected momentswhen she was walking down the street and saw a man with a similar gait, when she was reading and a turn of phrase reminded her of his voice, when she looked at Marysia and saw, in the set of the girl's jaw or the shape of her hands, the ghost of the man he had been.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175 Tm
(It was natural. It was not a relapse. It was not a betrayal. It was the mind's way of processing a wound that had been sealed and was now, ten years later, being forced open again.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 155 Tm
(But the mind, Helena was learning, was not content to merely process. The mind, given enough time and enough unresolved material, began to construct narratives. It began to ask questions that had no safe answers. What if he had not chosen his family? What if he had followed her to Poland? What if the war had not come, or had come differently, or had not found them? These questions were not useful. They were not productive. They were the mind's way of filling the silence with fiction, and Helena was too disciplinedtoo hardened by four years of occupationto indulge in fiction.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 135 Tm
(And yet.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 115 Tm
(And yet.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 75 Tm
(He came to the café again. Two weeks after their meeting, on a Tuesday, she walked in at noon and found him already there, at the same table, with two cups of coffee and a look of such naked hope that she felt her carefully constructed defenses shiver.) Tj
