(They met every day for two weeks. The café became their placenot exclusively, not obviously, but with the quiet regularity of a ritual that neither party acknowledged but both parties needed. They talked. They talked about everything and nothingthe war, obliquely, in the careful coded language of the occupied; the city, which they both knew intimately but from completely different perspectives; the weather, the food, the small absurdities of daily life that persisted even in the worst of times. They talked around the center of thingsthe love, the child, the decade of silence circling it like planets circling a star they could not approach without burning.) Tj
