96 0 obj
<< /Length 7095 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 535 Tm
(She emerged onto the street. She set Marysia down. The girl was shaking but silent, her face grey with sewer-water and fear. Helena knelt in front of her and held her face in her hands.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 515 Tm
("Are you hurt?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 495 Tm
("No.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 475 Tm
("Can you walk?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 455 Tm
("Yes.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 435 Tm
("Then walk. Walk with me. We're going to find a place to stay. We're going to") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 415 Tm
("Mama. Where's") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 395 Tm
(Helena closed her eyes. She held Marysia's face. She pressed her forehead against her daughter's forehead. She breathed.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 375 Tm
("He's brave," she said. "He's the bravest man I've ever known. And he" She stopped. She couldn't say it. She couldn't say the word that meant gone, that meant lost, that meant the thing you love most in the world has been taken from you and will not come back. "He's gone, kochanie. He stayed so we could get out. He") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 355 Tm
(Marysia began to cry. It was the first time she had cried since the roundupthe first time the dam had broken, the first time the nine-year-old girl had allowed herself the luxury of grief. She cried against her mother's shoulder, her small body convulsing with the force of it, and Helena held her and rocked her and wept with her, the two of them kneeling in the street in the dying city, crying for a man who had spent ten years running from love and ten years running back to it and who had, in the end, found it not in a lecture hall or a garden or a narrow bed, but in a sewer under a burning city, where he had stayed so that the woman he loved and the daughter he had never known could walk into the light.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315 Tm
(Jochen did not die in the sewer.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 295 Tm
(She learned this three days later, from a resistance fighter who had been in the tunnel system and who had witnessed the aftermath. The patrol had been a German cleaning unitsoldiers tasked with clearing the sewers of Polish fighters who used them as escape routes. Jochen had fought thema single man against five, armed with a pipe and a flashlight and the desperate strength of a man with something to protect. He had killed two of them. He had wounded another. The remaining two had shot himthree rounds, center mass, the efficient violence of trained soldiers dispatching a threat.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 275 Tm
(But he had not died immediately. He had lain in the sewer water, bleeding, and a Polish resistance fightersomeone who was moving through the tunnels on his own missionhad found him. The fighter had recognized the uniform under the civilian clothes. He had raised his weapon to finish the job.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 255 Tm
(And Jochen had spoken. In Polish. In the Polish he had learned from a woman he loved, in the language of the country he had helped occupy, in the tongue of the people he had served for four years as an officer of the occupying power. He had said: Tell Helena Wojtera. On ulica Mokotowska. Tell her I lived long enough to choose correctly. Tell her) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 235 Tm
(The fighter had not heard the rest. Jochen had died in the sewer, in the dark, in the water, with a Polish name on his lips and a Polish woman in his heart and the first truly uncowardly act of his life still echoing in the tunnel like a sound that refuses to stop.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 215 Tm
(The fighter found Helena in Praga. He told her what he had seen. He told her what Jochen had said. And HelenaHelena who had not wept since the courtyard, who had held herself rigid through the loss of her cell, the destruction of her city, the flight through the sewerHelena broke. She broke in the way that a riverbank breaks when the water rises too high: not with a crash but with a slow, total collapse, the earth giving way, the structure dissolving, everything that had been held back pouring out in a flood of grief that was also, in its deepest and most hidden layer, relief.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 195 Tm
(He had chosen correctly. The words echoed in her mind as she walked through the ruined streets of Praga, holding Marysia's hand, moving through the landscape of a city that was being systematically destroyed around her. He had chosen correctly. Not his family. Not his name. Not his safety. Not the Reich. Not the uniform. He had chosen her. He had chosen the child. He had chosen the thing that was true over the thing that was easy, and he had done it at the end, in the last possible moment, when the choice mattered most.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175 Tm
(It was not enough to undo the ten years. It was not enough to erase the war, or the occupation, or the child raised in secrecy, or the flat destroyed, or the friends taken, or the city burning. It was not enough to bring back the dead, or heal the wounded, or restore what had been lost. But it was enough to mean something. It was enough to be the final note in a symphony that had been dissonant for a decadea resolution, not into happiness, but into truth.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 135 Tm
(They survived. She and Marysia survived. They survived the Uprising, the German retaliation, the winter of 1944, and the final liberation in January 1945 when the Soviet army entered the ruins of Warsaw and the survivors emerged from cellars and sewers and hiding places like creatures emerging from a long hibernation, blinking in the light, trying to remember what the world looked like when it wasn't on fire.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 115 Tm
(Warsaw was destroyed. Eighty-five percent of the city was rubble. The streets were unrecognizable. The buildings were skeletons. The Vistula still flowed, indifferent, carrying its dark water past the wreckage of a civilization that had been, five years earlier, one of the great capitals of Europe and was now a graveyard of stone and memory.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 95 Tm
(Helena and Marysia lived in a refugee camp on the outskirts of the city. They lived in a single room with twelve other people. They ate what the army gave themthin soup, dark bread, the occasional piece of meat of uncertain origin. They waited. They survived. They breathed.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 75 Tm
(The years after the war were a different kind of wara war against silence, against forgetting, against the temptation to let the past stay buried and simply move forward as though the earth had not been saturated with blood. Helena moved forward. She had no choice. The new governmentthe Soviet-backed government that replaced the occupationdemanded forward motion, demanded reconstruction, demanded the performance of optimism in a landscape of rubble. She worked. She found employment in a government officetranslating documents, of all things, her German finally useful in a country that was now being administrated by a different set of occupiers who spoke a different language but maintained, in their own way, the same structures of control. She did not speak of the resistance. She did not speak of Jochen. She did not speak of the child who had been conceived in love and born in silence and raised in a city that had tried to destroy everything she held dear.) Tj
ET
