stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 545 Tm
(She spoke of nothing. She performed the role of the survivorthe competent, efficient, emotionally contained survivor who had lost everything and rebuilt from nothing and who did not require sympathy or understanding or the particular form of attention that the recently bereaved attract like flowers attract bees. She was, in the eyes of her colleagues, a woman of admirable resilience. She was, in the eyes of the few friends she maintained, a woman of impenetrable silence. She was, in her own eyes, a woman carrying an interior landscape so complicated, so contradictory, so laden with unresolved grief and impossible love that she could not begin to map it, much less share it.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 525 Tm
(Marysia grew. She grew in the post-war Warsaw of rationing and reconstruction, of new buildings rising from old rubble, of a city that was trying, with the desperate energy of the convalescent, to pretend it was healthy. She went to schoola real school, with a satchel and textbooks and a teacher who taught history and geography and the things that the occupation had tried to erase. She made friends. She played in the streets. She laughed, sometimesa sound that was like music in Helena's ears, the sound of a childhood that was, finally, beginning to resemble something normal.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 505 Tm
(But Marysia was not normal. She could not be normal. The war had shaped her, as it shaped every child of her generation, in ways that were invisible to the casual observer but that would, over time, reveal themselves in the architecture of her personalityin her wariness, her self-reliance, her refusal to take safety for granted, her habit of counting the exits in every room she entered. She was a child of the occupation, and the occupation was in her bones, in her blood, in the particular quality of her attention that was always, always, scanning the environment for threats that might or might not be there.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 485 Tm
(Helena watched this and grieved. She grieved for the childhood her daughter had been denied, for the innocence that had been stolen, for the normal life that had been replaced by something harder and sharper and less forgiving. And she grieved, in the private chambers of her heart, for the man who had given Marysia those eyesthose dark, steady, assessing eyes that were both hers and his, and that carried in their depths the genetic memory of a love that the world had tried to destroy and had, in its typically clumsy and violent way, failed.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 465 Tm
(She thought of Jochen every day. Not in the sharp, agonizing way of the first monthsthe way of a wound still fresh, still bleeding, still demanding attentionbut in the dull, persistent way of a scar. The scar was there. It would always be there. It ached sometimesin certain light, in certain weather, in certain moments of unexpected beauty or unexpected lossbut it did not bleed. It was part of her. It was the landscape of her body, as permanent and as unremarkable as the shape of her hand or the color of her eyes.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 445 Tm
(Sometimes, in the small hours of the night, when Marysia was asleep and the flat was quiet and the city was quiet and the world was quiet, Helena allowed herself to remember. She remembered the seminar room. The library. The English Garden. The bridge. The flat on Amalienstrasse. The sexthe terrible, beautiful, consuming sex that had been the physical expression of an emotional truth too large for words. She remembered his hands, his mouth, the way he had held her, the way he had said her name. She remembered the morning afterthe grey light, the cold flat, the feeling of being known in a way that was both terrifying and the most natural thing in the world.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 425 Tm
(She remembered these things and she did not weep. Weeping was for the early years. Now, in the later years, in the years of scar tissue and silence and the slow accumulation of a life that was, if not happy, then at least lived, she remembered with a kind of dry, clear-eyed tenderness that was its own form of lovenot the passionate, desperate, world-ending love of the early days, but something quieter, something more durable, something that could survive the years the way a river survives the landscape: by flowing around the obstacles, by wearing down the stone, by continuing, always, to move forward.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 405 Tm
(And one daya day in late March, when the first true warmth of spring touched the air and the sky was blue for the first time in what felt like yearsHelena sat on the steps of the refugee center and watched Marysia playing in the courtyard with the other children. The girl was laughing. She was chasing a ball. She was nine years old, and she was alive, and she was free, and she was, for the first time in her young life, simply a child.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 385 Tm
(Helena watched her. She felt the sun on her face. She felt the warmth of the step beneath her. She felt, in her chest, the coal of memorythe memory of a seminar room in Munich, a garden in October, a bridge over a river, a narrow bed in a cold flat, a sewer in a burning city, a man's voice saying her name for the last time in the dark.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 365 Tm
(She took out a piece of papera scrap, a remnant, something she had found in the rubble of the flat on ulica Mokotowska, preserved by the accident of being under a fallen beam when the rest of the room was destroyed. It was a page from a book. A page from Heine. She had carried it with her through the Uprising, through the sewer, through the refugee camp. It was all she had left of the life she had lived before the warnot the Munich life, not the resistance life, but the love life, the life that had existed in the spaces between the other lives, the life that was hers and his and that no war could entirely destroy.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 345 Tm
(She read the page. The words were faded, the ink brown with age, but she could read themshe could read them because she had read them a thousand times, in a flat in Munich, in a library in Warsaw, in a cellar during a roundup, in a sewer under a dying city:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 325 Tm
(In the lovely month of May,) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315 Tm
(when all the buds were springing,) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 305 Tm
(then it was that love came into my heart.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 285 Tm
(She folded the page. She put it back in her pocket. She watched Marysia playing in the courtyard. The sun was warm. The sky was blue. The Vistula flowed. And Helena Wojterathe woman who had loved across the wreckage of the world, who had carried a child in silence, who had survived a war and a love and a loss that would have broken anyone with less iron in her soulsat on the steps of a refugee center in the ruins of Warsaw and, for the first time in a very long time, allowed herself to hope.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 265 Tm
(Not for the future. She had learned, in the school of the occupation, that hope for the future was a luxury that the world did not generally grant. She hoped for nothing so specific, so vulnerable, so easily disappointed.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 245 Tm
(She hoped, simply, that the river would remember. That the Vistula, in its slow and ancient passage toward the sea, would carry with it the memory of the things that had happened on its banksthe love, the loss, the violence, the tenderness, the man who had chosen correctly in the end, the woman who had loved him despite everything, the child who had called him papa in a courtyard in a burning city. That the water would hold these things, the way water holds everythinggently, indifferently, permanentlyand would carry them forward into a future that would, eventually, forget the war and remember only the river, and the people who had loved beside it, and the truth that love, even love across the wreckage of the world, is not nothing. Is never nothing. Is, in the end, the only thing that is.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 225 Tm
(She sat in the sun. She watched her daughter play. She breathed.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 205 Tm
(And the river flowed on.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 145 Tm
(Warsaw, 1965.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 125 Tm
(Marysia was twenty-two years old. She was studying history at the University of Warsawthe same university where her grandfather had taught, where her mother had once been a student before the war interrupted everything. She was tall, dark-haired, with the steady eyes of a woman who had seen too much as a child and had spent the intervening years learning how to look at the world without flinching.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 105 Tm
(She sat in the living room of the flat on ulica Mokotowskathe rebuilt flat, the new flat, the flat that had been reconstructed from the rubble and the memory of what had been there before. The wallpaper was new. The stove was new. The window looked out over a street that had been, twenty-two years ago, a battlefield and was now, once again, a street where people walked and children played and the ordinary business of living resumed its ancient, persistent course.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 85 Tm
(Helena was in the kitchen. She was fifty-seven years old. Her hair was grey. Her face was lined. Her handsstill strong, still precisemoved through the business of making tea with the automatic grace of long practice. She heard Marysia's footsteps in the hall and turned as her daughter appeared in the doorway.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 65 Tm
("Mama. I found something.") Tj
