1 0 0 1 50 535 Tm
("What did you find?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 515 Tm
(Marysia held out a piece of paper. It was oldyellowed, brittle, the edges crumbling. It was a document. A German document. A military record.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 495 Tm
("It was in the archives," Marysia said. "I was researching the occupation for my thesis. I was looking at the personnel records of the Wehrmacht administration in Warsaw, and I found") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 475 Tm
(She handed the paper to Helena. Helena took it. She looked at it.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 455 Tm
(It was a discharge form. A military discharge, dated March 1944. The name: Oberleutnant Jochen von Brandt. The reason for discharge: KIA. Warsaw Uprising. Sewer operations.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 435 Tm
(And in the margin, in a different hand, a note: Personal effects returned to The rest was illegible. Smudged. Lost.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 415 Tm
(Helena held the paper. She stared at it. The kitchen was very quiet. The tea kettle began to whistle.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 395 Tm
("Mama?") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 375 Tm
("I'm alright." Helena set the paper down on the table. She turned off the kettle. She poured the tea. Her hands were steady. Her face was composed. Inside, the coal of memory burnedbrighter now, hotter, the accumulated heat of twenty-two years of silence finally finding, in this scrap of paper, a voice.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 355 Tm
("He was your father," Helena said.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 335 Tm
("I know.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315 Tm
("I never told you") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 295 Tm
("I know, Mama. I've always known.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 275 Tm
(Helena looked at her daughter. Marysia's eyes were steady. The eyes that were hers and histhe eyes that held in their dark depths the genetic argument of two people who had loved each other across the wreckage of the world and had, in the end, found their way back to each other, if not in life, then in the only country that outlasts life: the country of memory, of water, of a river that flows on and on and remembers everything the world tries to forget.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 255 Tm
("What do we do with it?" Marysia asked.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 235 Tm
(Helena looked at the paper. She looked at her daughter. She looked out the window at the streetthe rebuilt street, the living street, the street where children played and the Vistula flowed and the world continued its ancient business of destruction and renewal, destruction and renewal, without end.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 215 Tm
("We keep it," she said. "We keep it the way the river keeps things. Gently. Indifferently. Permanently. We keep it because it's true. And because the trutheven the hard truth, even the truth that hurtsis all we have that the war couldn't take away.") Tj
1 0 0 1 50 195 Tm
(She picked up the paper. She folded it carefully. She put it in her pocket, next to the other scrapthe page from Heine that she had carried for twenty-two years, the page that had survived the Uprising and the sewer and the refugee camp and the long slow reconstruction of a life from nothing.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175 Tm
(Two scraps of paper. Two pieces of a story that the world would never fully know. Two fragments of a love that had been, against all odds and all reason, the most real thing in a world devoted to unreality.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 155 Tm
(She poured the tea. She sat with her daughter. They drank in silence. And outside, the Vistula floweddark, ancient, indifferent, carrying with it the memory of a city, a war, a love, and a man who had, in the end, chosen correctly.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 135 Tm
(The river remembered.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 115 Tm
(The river always remembers.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 75 Tm
(FIN) Tj
